45	\begin{document}
46	\title{\textbf{The DQI Circuit for Optimal Polynomial Intersection as the Classical-Logical Image of a Paraconsistent Word}}
47	\author{C.\ Lando Mills\\
48	Trabuco Canyon, California\\
49	\texttt{c.landonmills@gmail.com}\\
50	ORCID: \href{https://orcid.org/0000-0003-0003-0552}{0000-0003-0003-0552}}
51	\date{1 September 2026}
52	\maketitle
53	
54	\begin{abstract}
55	Quantum mechanics is not a second reading of the Belnap/FDE structure standing
56	beside a paraconsistent one; it is what remains once that structure is
57	evaluated classically instead. We establish this directionally, in three
58	independent ways, for Decoded Quantum Interferometry (DQI) applied to Optimal
59	Polynomial Intersection (OPI). \emph{Theoretically}: the classical restriction
60	of Belnap FOUR is a coreflector — an ex falso default of the paradox state $B$
61	and the gap state $N$ onto falsity — proven in Lean with zero sorries; the
62	dual reflector, defaulting to truth, is proven \emph{not} to exist as an
63	adjunction in the same order, and the paraconsistent closure operator whose
64	fixed point is $B$ is proven inexpressible inside either classical
65	restriction. \emph{Constructively}: we exhibit and mechanically verify a
66	paraconsistent IMASM word realizing FDE-trilattice-derived DQI, closing under
67	every rotation of its own orbit, where the word as auto-designed did not.
68	\emph{Empirically}: we compute DQI's own two closed forms for OPI's
69	satisfaction fraction — Jordan et al.'s exact finite-$m$ eigenvalue expression
70	and its $m\to p$ asymptotic limit — directly, watching the richer,
71	operator-valued exact answer collapse monotonically onto the poorer scalar
72	limit across four real scales, the same directional loss the Lean theorems
73	establish abstractly. Neither of DQI's own closed forms asserts anything
74	back to the other; one is what remains of the paraconsistent word once the
75	proven collapse is applied at finite size, the other is what remains at the
76	limit.
77	\end{abstract}
78	
79	\section{The central claim}
80	\label{sec:1}
81	
82	\begin{center}
83	\begin{tabular}{ll}
84	FDE evaluated paraconsistently & $\longrightarrow$ FDE proper \\[4pt]
85	FDE evaluated through classical logic & $\longrightarrow$ QM
86	\end{tabular}
87	\end{center}
88	
89	Quantum mechanics arises when the underlying FDE/Belnap structure is
90	evaluated classically rather than paraconsistently. A DQI circuit is
91	therefore the classical-logical image of a paraconsistent IMASM word on the
92	Belnap/SIXTEEN$_3$ trilattice; its apparent quantum behavior is produced by
93	the loss of the native paraconsistent evaluation, not by any additional
94	physical ingredient DQI supplies. This is a directional relationship, not a
95	symmetric one: the two sides do not assert facts about each other, because
96	only one of them is derived from the other. \Cref{sec:collapse} states the
97	direction Lean actually proves; \Cref{sec:word} exhibits and verifies a
98	concrete paraconsistent object of the stated class; \Cref{sec:opi}
99	onward measure what the classical image of that class looks like for one
100	specific problem, Optimal Polynomial Intersection.
101	
102	\section{The classical collapse, formally}
103	\label{sec:collapse}
104	
105	Four files in \texttt{p4ramill/Imscribing/Paraconsistent/} carry this
106	section, all machine-checked, all zero sorries:
107	\texttt{ParaconsistentFrobeniusClosure.lean}, \texttt{DeMorganBooleanCentre.lean},
108	\texttt{ClassicalRestriction.lean}, \texttt{FalsifiabilityDegenerate.lean}.
109	
110	\begin{definition}[Belnap FOUR]
111	$\mathbf 4 = \{N,T,F,B\}$ with negation $\neg N = N$, $\neg T = F$, $\neg F =
112	T$, $\neg B = B$, and truth order $F <_t N <_t T$, $F <_t B <_t T$, with $N$
113	and $B$ incomparable.
114	\end{definition}
115	
116	\begin{theorem}[The paraconsistent closure lands entirely outside the classical fragment]
117	\label{thm:inc}
118	The closure operator $\operatorname{inc} : \mathbf 4 \to \mathbf 4$ is
119	constant at $B$ (\texttt{inc\_always\_B}), hence idempotent
120	(\texttt{frobenius\_closure}: $\operatorname{inc}(\operatorname{inc}(s)) =
121	\operatorname{inc}(s)$ for every $s$), with $B$ its unique fixed point
122	(\texttt{only\_B\_is\_fixed\_point}).
123	\end{theorem}
124	
125	\begin{theorem}[The classical restriction is a coreflector, and only a coreflector]
126	\label{thm:coreflector}
127	Let $\text{boolSwitch} : \mathbf 4 \to \mathbf 4$ send $T \mapsto T$ and
128	everything else to $F$ — an ex falso default: absent or contradictory
129	truth-commitment ($N$, $B$) both default to falsity, never to truth. Then:
130	\begin{enumerate}
131	\item $\text{boolSwitch}$ is a genuine coreflector: $\iota \dashv
132	\text{boolSwitch}$ is a coreflective adjunction of the Boolean centre
133	$\{T,F\}$ into $\mathbf 4$ in the truth order
134	(\texttt{\seqsplit{centre\_coreflective\_in\_truth\_order}}).
135	\item The dual direction — a reflector defaulting $N,B$ to $T$ — is
136	\emph{not} a second, equally valid option: no such adjunction exists in the
137	truth order (\texttt{\seqsplit{truth\_switch\_left\_adjoint\_fails}}).
138	\item Neither direction is an adjunction in the information order at all
139	(\texttt{\seqsplit{info\_inclCentre\_left\_adjoint\_fails}}).
140	\end{enumerate}
141	The same shape holds one level up, for the three-valued restriction
142	$\{N,T,F\}$ that removes only $B$
143	(\texttt{classicalSwitch}, $B \mapsto F$,
144	\texttt{\seqsplit{classical\_coreflective\_in\_truth\_order}}, \texttt{ClassicalRestriction.lean}).
145	\end{theorem}
146	
147	\begin{theorem}[The collapse is exactly lossy]
148	\label{thm:inexpressible}
149	$\operatorname{inc}$ of \Cref{thm:inc} cannot be formulated inside either
150	classical restriction: there is no $f$ on the classical fragment with
151	$\iota(f(c)) = \operatorname{inc}(\iota(c))$ for every $c$
152	(\texttt{frobenius\_closure\_inexpressible\_classically}), because
153	$\operatorname{inc}$'s image is $\{B\}$ and $B$ is excluded from the
154	classical fragment by construction (\texttt{inc\_lands\_outside\_classical}).
155	$B$ is not merely absent from the classical image; the theorem that produces
156	it cannot even be \emph{stated} inside the restriction.
157	\end{theorem}
158	
