$$



Once you make your diagonal \(kd\) derivation explicit, I would actually add a small self-loop to each weight sector labeled \(kd\). Then the figure literally becomes the tridiagonal matrix: forward edge \(a_{k+1}\), backward edge \(a_{k+1}\), self-loop \(kd\). That could be exceptionally clean.

A third good visual is the coordinate-wise coreflector. Right now the prose does something mathematically important rather quickly: \(B_4\subset4\) becomes \(B_4^m\subset4^{\odot m}\), and the Boolean image is identified with \(\mathbb F_2^m\).  A commuting diagram would make that transition much easier to digest:

```tex
\[
\begin{tikzcd}[column sep=large,row sep=large]
4^{\odot m}
  \arrow[r, shift left=.7ex, "R^{m}"]
&
B_4^{m}
  \arrow[l, shift left=.7ex, "\iota^{m}"]
  \arrow[r, "\cong"]
&
\{F,T\}^{m}
  \arrow[r, "\cong"]
&
\mathbb F_2^{m}
\\
4
  \arrow[u, "\Delta_m"]
  \arrow[r, shift left=.7ex, "R"]
&
B_4
  \arrow[l, shift left=.7ex, "\iota"]
  \arrow[u, "\Delta_m"']
\end{tikzcd}
\]
```

I would label underneath it:

```tex
\[
\iota \dashv R
\quad\Longrightarrow\quad
\iota^m \dashv R^m.
\]
```

That would be particularly effective if you move the coordinate-wise lifting argument into this paper, as you're apparently doing.

Another useful figure would explicitly separate the two collapses, because that distinction protects you against an obvious criticism:

```tex
\begin{figure}[t]
\centering
\begin{tikzpicture}[
 box/.style={draw, rounded corners, align=center, inner sep=7pt},
 arr/.style={->, thick},
 node distance=24mm
]
\node[box] (p) {Paraconsistent register\\$4^{\odot m}$};
\node[box, right=of p] (b) {Boolean image\\$B_4^m\cong\mathbb F_2^m$};

\node[box, below=18mm of p] (a)
 {Finite operator\\$A(m,\ell,d)$};
\node[box, right=of a] (phi)
 {Asymptotic scalar\\$\phi_{\rm DQI}$};

\draw[arr] (p) -- node[above] {coreflection} (b);
\draw[arr] (a) -- node[above] {$m\to p$} (phi);

\node[below=5mm of b, align=center]
 {\footnotesize logical/categorical loss};
\node[below=5mm of phi, align=center]
 {\footnotesize asymptotic/operator loss};
\end{tikzpicture}
\caption{Two directional reductions used in the argument.
They exhibit the same richer-to-poorer orientation but are
different mathematical maps.}
\end{figure}
```

I especially like that one because it heads off the objection that the paper conflates coreflection with taking an asymptotic limit. Your empirical section describes the finite operator converging toward the scalar limit, while the formal section describes an entirely different restriction of FOUR. 

And since you already have numerical data, I would absolutely turn Table 1 into a tiny `pgfplots` figure as well. Four points are enough because the visual statement is simple:

```tex
\begin{figure}[t]
\centering
\begin{tikzpicture}
\begin{axis}[
  width=.78\linewidth,
  height=5.2cm,
  xmode=log,
  xlabel={$p$},
  ylabel={$\langle s\rangle/p$},
  legend pos=south east,
  grid=major
]
\addplot+[mark=*] coordinates {
  (101,0.8938)
  (1009,0.9229)
  (2003,0.9259)
  (10007,0.9296)
};
\addlegendentry{finite $m$}

\addplot+[mark=square*] coordinates {
  (101,0.9316)
  (1009,0.9317)
  (2003,0.9316)
  (10007,0.9316)
};
\addlegendentry{$m\to p$ limit}
\end{axis}
\end{tikzpicture}
\caption{Finite-$m$ DQI satisfaction approaching its
asymptotic scalar value at fixed $n/p\approx0.495$.}
\end{figure}
```

Those are exactly the four reported values, so the figure adds no new claim. 

If I were choosing only two because the paper is short, I'd use the weight-sector ladder and the one-object chain. The ladder is the mathematical money shot; the chain tells the reader what the entire paper claims. Once your \(kd\) derivation is in place, I can also sketch a more polished ladder figure where the graph itself is literally \(A(m,\ell,d)\), including the diagonal loops.

